Applying Kirchhoff's rules to \cref{FigureNOR-GATE} leads to
%
$ C\frac{\dd \vout}{\dd t} = \frac{\vdd-\vout}{R_1(t)+R_2(t)} -
\frac{\vout}{R_3(t)\ ||\ R_4(t)}\ .$
%
This can be transformed to the
non-homogeneous ordinary differential equation (ODE) with non-constant coefficients
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq0}
\frac{\dd V_{out}}{\dd t}+\frac{V_{out}}{C\,R_g(t)}=U(t),
\end{equation}
using
$\frac{1}{R_g(t)}=\frac{1}{R_1(t)+R_2(t)}+\frac{1}{R_3(t)}+\frac{1}{R_4(t)}$ and
$U(t)=\frac{V_{DD}}{C(R_1(t)+R_2(t))}$. Note that the entire voltage divider in
\cref{FigureNOR-GATE} is equivalent to an ideal voltage source
$U_0=\vdd\frac{R_3(t)||R_4(t)}{R_1(t)+R_2(t)+R_3(t)||R_4(t)}$ and a serial
resistor $R_g(t)$ sourcing $C$. Consequently, $CU(t)=U_0/R_g(t)$ in \cref{Eq0}
is the short-circuit current, and $CU(t)-\vout/R_g(t)$ the current actually
sourced into $C$. It is well-known that the general
solution of \eqref{Eq0} is
\begin{equation}
\label{Eq1}
    V_{out}(t)= V_0\ e^{-G(t)} + \int_{0}^{t} U(s)\ e^{G(s)-G(t)}\dd s,
\end{equation}
where $V_0=V_{out}(0)$ denotes the initial condition and $G(t) = \int_{0}^{t}
(C\,R_g(s))^{-1} \dd s$.

