Parametrizing our model for these \SI{65}{\nm} MIS delays turned out to be remarkable easy:
Exactly as for our \SI{15}{\nm} technology, by initially fixing some value for the load capacitance $C$ and trying to match $\delta_{M,-}^{\downarrow}(-\infty)$ (or $\delta_{M,+}^{\downarrow}(\infty)$) and $\delta_{M,+}^{\downarrow}(0)$ with $\ddoD_S(-\infty)-\dmin$ (or $\ddoD_S(\infty)-\dmin$) and $\ddoD_S(0)-\dmin$, we determined accurate values for $R_{n_A}$ and $R_{n_B}$. After fixing the latter, we obtained the values of the remaining parameters by fitting the remaining delay values.  Note carefully, however, that we had to use a different pure delay $\dmin=\SI{10.8}{\ps}$ here, since $\frac{\dsd(-\infty)}{\dsd(0)} \approx \frac{\SI{222}{\ps}}{\SI{116}{\ps}}$. \cref{table:params65} provides the resulting list of parameters. Not surprisingly, since the gate delay is considerably higher than for our \SI{15}{\nm} data, we indeed face a significantly larger value for the load capacitance $C$ also in our model.

