\section{Result analysis}
\label{section4}
We relied on our methodology to perform citation analysis. In the remainder of this section, we further discuss the results of that analysis in the light of our three research questions.

\subsection{RQ1: gender distribution in
SE journals authorship}
We completed Steps 1 to 4 of our methodology. This allowed us to use the resulting 96,282 papers to plot the authorship trends in the SE literature between 2009 and 2024. Figure  \ref{fig:authorship_trends} shows that chart. Hence, Figure \ref{fig:authorship_trends}  illustrates the expected proportions of papers authored in each gender category. That Figure shows that, in the SE literature, the MM category dominates the authorship while the WW category is underrepresented. Still, over time, more papers have been authored by authors belonging to the WW category. Hence, between 2009 and 2024, the WW authorship has increased by nearly 2.3\% in the SE field. But, as of August 2024, the authorship in the SE field remains little diversified since the MM authorship still represents two-thirds of the authorship while the WW authorship only represents 5.5\% of the authorship.

%\subsubsection{Women tend to produce less publications than men overtime} \cite{b44, b29}: as Figure \ref{fig:authorship_trends} shows, women usually produce less publications than men overtime. This is in accordance with Wu \cite{b44} and Tahamtan \cite{b29} who both stated that women usually produce less papers than men over the course of  their careers. This pattern probably results from \textcolor{red}{career  interruptions, and family caretaker's responsibilities}. Due to this pattern, women have less opportunities to be cited and also have  less impact on gendered citation practices. 

