\subsubsection{The number of negative samples K} The experimental results in Table V show that using 16 negative samples K achieves almost the best MRR and Hit@1 performance on DB15K and MKG-W datasets. Although increasing K to 32 or 64 slightly improves the performance, the gain is minimal and the server resource consumption increases significantly. More negative samples can stabilize representation learning by better distinguishing adversarial noise and enhancing modality discrimination. However, too many negative samples increase the computational overhead and the performance improvement weakens as the negative pairs become more similar. Therefore, a moderate amount of negative samples is the best choice to balance performance and efficiency.


