\subsection{Representational Transition Zone}

% 首先说明互信息与熵的等式，指出正常神经网络随深度加深伴随的信息传递会起到降低I(x,z)的作用，这样就达到了抬高H(x|z)与H(z)的效果，从而增加了MIA的难度。但是，I(x,z)的下降水平主要也与神经网络的设计、信息承载上限的损失强相关。所以在神经网络浅层，中间层信息承载信息量大，互信息高，是很容易因MIA达到良好的输入数据重构效果的。

The mutual information between the input \( x \) and an intermediate representation \( z \) is defined as:
\begin{equation}
I(x, z) = H(x) - H(x|z) = H(z) - H(z|x)
\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}
When the mapping $F_{\text{edge}}: x \mapsto z$ is deterministic, as in standard feedforward networks~\cite{xia2025theoretical}, $H(z|x) = 0$, and thus $I(x, z) = H(z)$.

In standard feedforward networks, feature transformations form a Markov chain, and by the data processing inequality:
\begin{equation}
I(x, z_{\ell1}) \geq I(x, z_{\ell2}) \geq \cdots \geq I(x, z_{\ell})
\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}
implying that $I(x,z)$ decreases with depth, corresponding to increased $H(x|z)$ and reduced $H(z)$, which hinders MIA success~\cite{2025arXiv250100824L}.

However, the rate at which $I(x, z)$ decreases depends strongly on the network architecture and the representational capacity of each layer. In shallow or intermediate layers, high mutual information persists, leaving them vulnerable to inversion. 

% 不过同时，也说明直观印象中模型越深越能抵抗攻击的观点是存在问题的。如图1所示，该被攻击模型为经典的ViT6层深架构，该模型中间层输出部分始终保持着单维度的决策信息向量与256维的特征信息向量结合。即使是攻击到第6层的输出，模型的输出依然能保持(257,128)的高维、大信息量负载的效果，MIA依旧能起到理想的信息重构效果。这一特例是目前该领域研究未提及并做相关防御机制设计的架构，我们在此加深一个观点的传输，即模型深度越深抵抗MIA的效果体现在于I(x,z)有所下降，影响H(x|z),H(z)，但互信息下降的水平也同样需要达到抵抗MIA的效果。

So, we challenge the common belief that deeper models are inherently more resistant to inversion attacks. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:vit}, even attacking the 6th (final) layer of a 6-layer Vision Transformer (ViT)~\cite{dosovitskiy2020image} yields accurate reconstructions. Each layer retains a hybrid representation (1D decision token + 256D patch embeddings), producing a high-dimensional output of shape \( (257, 128) \) rich in instance-specific information. Despite the increased depth, the high-dimensional structure preserves the upper bound of \( H(z) \), limiting the reduction in mutual information \( I(x; z) \). According to Equation~\ref{eq:1}, the impact on \( I(x; z) \) and the conditional entropy \( H(x|z) \) is therefore marginal, leading to weak resistance against model inversion attacks.


With a properly designed inversion model, we achieve high-fidelity reconstructions from the final layer---a result not reported in prior work~\cite{chen2024dia}. The model architecture and training code are detailed in Appendix. This underscores our key insight: depth alone is insufficient; only a significant reduction in mutual information can ensure inversion resistance. If the model is partitioned at this point, the defense no longer requires complex mechanisms.

\begin{definition}[Differential Entropy~\cite{shannon1948mathematical}]\label{def:2.1}
Let $X$ be a continuous random vector in $\mathbb R^d$ with probability density function $f_X(x)$.  Its \emph{differential entropy} is defined as
\[
  h(X)\;=\;- \int_{\mathbb R^d} f_X(x)\,\ln f_X(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,
\]
provided the integral exists.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Maximum Entropy Principle for Fixed Covariance~\cite{jaynes1957information}]\label{lem:2.2}
Among all continuous distributions on $\mathbb R^d$ with a given covariance matrix $\Sigma$, the Gaussian distribution $\mathcal N(\mu,\Sigma)$ uniquely maximizes the differential entropy.  In particular, for any $X$ with $\mathrm{Cov}(X)=\Sigma$,
\[
  h(X)\;\le\;h\bigl(\mathcal N(\mu,\Sigma)\bigr)
  \;=\;\tfrac12\ln\!\bigl[(2\pi e)^d\det\Sigma\bigr].
\]
\end{lemma}

% ——— Transitional sentence inserted here ———
In the following analysis, we will make use of Definition~\ref{def:2.1} and Lemma~\ref{lem:2.2} to derive upper bounds on the differential entropy of both feature‐level and decision‐level activations.


Non-generative neural networks consistently exhibit a clustering behavior whereby intermediate representations collapse into a “decision region” that predominantly encodes class information.  If we let \(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\in\mathbb{R}^D\) denote the random variable corresponding to these decision-level activations, then its overall entropy satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
&h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\bigr)
= h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\mid Y\bigr) \;+\; I\bigl(Y;Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\bigr) \le h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\mid Y\bigr) \;+\; H(Y) \\
&\le \max_{c}h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\mid Y=c\bigr)\;+\;H(Y)\le \max_{c}h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\mid Y=c\bigr)\;+\;\ln K
\end{aligned}
\]

