    \Description{Comparison and visualization of mean squared error and peak signal-to-noise ratio after performing MIA on different depths of ViT (a total of 6 transformer blocks).}
    \label{fig:vit}
\end{figure}

Next, we turn to the feature‐level representations. Let $Z_{\mathrm{feat}}\in\mathbb{R}^d$ denote the continuous random vector of intermediate features at some layer. By Lemma 2.2, among all distributions on \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with covariance \(\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}\), 
\[
h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{feat}}\bigr)
\le
h\bigl(\mathcal{N}(\mu_{feat},\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}})\bigr)
= \frac12\ln\!\bigl[(2\pi e)^d\det\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}\bigr].
\]

Moreover, if we let $\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^2=\frac{1}{d}\,\mathrm{tr}\bigl(\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}\bigr)$, then by the determinant–trace inequality
\(\det\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}\le(\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^2)^d\) it follows that
\begin{equation}
h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{feat}}\bigr)
\le
\frac{d}{2}\,\ln\!\Bigl(2\pi e\,\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^2\Bigr)
= O\!\bigl(d\ln\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^2\bigr).
\label{eq:3}
\end{equation}

Similarly, for the decision‐level representation we approximate each class‐conditional distribution
\[
Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\mid Y=c \sim \mathcal{N}\bigl(\mu_c,\Sigma_c\bigr),
\]

By Lemma~2.2, and applying the orthogonal spectral decomposition of \(\Sigma_c\) together with the Arithmetic Mean – Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM) inequality~\cite{hardy1952inequalities}, we obtain
\begin{equation}
h\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\mid Y=c\bigr)
\le \frac{1}{2}\ln\!\bigl[(2\pi e)^D\det\Sigma_c\bigr]
\le \frac{D}{2}\ln\!\Bigl(2\pi e\,\frac{R_c^2}{D}\Bigr)    
\label{eq:4}
\end{equation}
where $R_c^2$ denote the within-class mean‐squared radius, i.e., $R_c^2 = \mathrm{tr}\bigl(\Sigma_c\bigr)
= \frac{1}{N_c}\sum_{i:y_i=c}\|z_i - \mu_c\|^2$. Refer to Appendix~\ref{appA} for the above calculation details.

Combining Eqs.~3 and 4 gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta h
&\equiv
h\!\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{feat}}\bigr)
-
h\!\bigl(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\bigr)
\\
&\le
\frac{d}{2}\,\ln\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^{2}
-
\frac{D}{2}\,\ln\!\Bigl(\tfrac{R_c^{2}}{D}\Bigr)
+\frac{d-D}{2}\,\ln(2\pi e)
-
\ln K .
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the gap depends on
\(d,\; D,\;\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}})\), and \(R_c^{2}\).
Typically \(d\!\ge\!D\) (often only 1–4 × larger), and
\(\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^{2} = \tfrac{1}{d}\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_{\mathrm{feat}})\)
contains both intra- and inter-class variance, whereas
\(R_c^{2} = \operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_c)\) reflects only the within-class
spread of class \(c\).  Consequently the first two logarithmic terms and the
positive constant \(\tfrac{d-D}{2}\ln(2\pi e)\) together create a sizeable
positive gap, while the \(\ln K\) correction is too small to offset it.

Although our theoretical bounds are formulated for the \emph{differential entropy} of continuous random vectors, the same phenomenon carries over to the \emph{Shannon entropy} of the finite-precision tensors actually stored at run time.  In practice, each activation vector \(Z\) is quantised to a discrete tensor \(z\), and entropies are estimated over mini-batches.  By the quantisation lemma of Cover–Thomas\,\cite[Thm.~12.3]{cover2006elements}, this discretisation changes the entropy only by an additive term that depends on the dimensionality and the quantisation step.  Hence the population-level drop in entropy from \(Z_{\mathrm{feat}}\) to \(Z_{\mathrm{dec}}\) remains—indeed is slightly amplified—when measured on individual activations \(z\).  A complete derivation is provided in Appendix~\ref{appB}.

% 我们可以发现神经网络的决策信息会以分类的形式表现，当神经网络传递的中间层信息能由特征表征转为决策表征的话，其H(z)应该有如下不等式关系

% 正常表征为特征信息时，H(z)是样本级别的丰富度，其熵上界可表达为O(d_feat)。严格来说，根据最大熵原理，可分析出如下具体的熵上界

% 而决策表征的信息熵上界为，落到R2c上，与H(z_dfeat)比小很多。因为R2c仅与c类挂钩，d_feat与全局相关，类间散度更大。所以该区域会有一个熵的断崖式下跌。

Using the upper bounds in Eqs.~\ref{eq:3} and \ref{eq:4} together with the information identity in Eq. \ref{eq:1}, we can derive a lower bound on $H(x|z)$, which quantifies the residual uncertainty of $x$ given $z$:
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
&H(x \mid z) 
= H(x)\;-\;I(x;z) \\
&\ge H(x)\;-\;H(z) 
(\text{regardless of whether } F_{\text{edge}}\text{ is deterministic})
\\
&\ge 
\begin{cases}
H(x)\;-\;\displaystyle\frac{d}{2}\,\ln\!\bigl(2\pi e\,\sigma_{\mathrm{feat}}^{2}\bigr),
& z \in Z_{\mathrm{feat}}, \\[10pt]
H(x)\;-\;\displaystyle\frac{D}{2}\,\ln\!\Bigl(2\pi e\,\tfrac{R_c^{2}}{D}\Bigr)-\text{ln}K,
& z \in Z_{\mathrm{dec}} .
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:5}
\end{equation}

Since \(H(x)\) is a constant in Eq.~\ref{eq:5}, the lower bound on \(H(x|z)\)
is dominated by the term that is subtracted from it.  When \(z\) is a
\emph{decision‐level} representation, this subtracted term
\(\tfrac{D}{2}\ln\!\bigl(2\pi e\,R_c^{2}/D\bigr)\)
is typically smaller than its feature‐level counterpart,
so the bound on \(H(x|z)\) is larger, i.e.\ there is more residual
uncertainty about the input.  In the decision layer this bound depends only
on the \(D\) and the \(R_c^{2}\).  As \(D\) is architectural and therefore constant during training, the key quantity we can influence is \(R_c^{2}\).
Accordingly, the next question we ask is: \emph{how does \(R_c^{2}\) evolve
through training, and can we deliberately control it?}

% 同样我们也可以得到等式 H(x|z)与R2c的关系

% 我们继续挖掘R2C在训练过程中的变化情况。发现CE训练后期半径几乎不变，并且在中期可能增大。

\subsection{Rethink Label Smoothing to MIA}
With cross-entropy training and one-hot targets, the back propagated feature
gradient is \(\tilde g_i = J_i^{\!\top}\delta_i\) with
\(\delta_i = p_i - y_i\) (see Table~\ref{tab:notation}).
An SGD update \(z_i^{+}=z_i-\gamma\,\tilde g_i\) therefore induces the
first-order change
\begin{equation}
\Delta R_c^{2}
= -\frac{2\gamma}{N_c}
  \sum_{i\in c}(z_i-\mu_c)^{\!\top}\tilde g_i
\label{eq:6}
\end{equation}
where the \(1/N_c\) factor appears because \(R_c^{2}\) is defined as a class
average.

Substituting
\(\tilde g_i = J_i^{\!\top}(p_i-y_i)\) gives
\begin{equation}
\Delta R_c^{2}(\text{one-hot})
= -\frac{2\gamma}{N_c}
  \sum_{i\in c}\Bigl[(p_{ic}-1)\,T_{\!\mathrm{corr},i}
                     +\sum_{k\neq c}p_{ik}\,T_{k,i}\Bigr]
\label{eq:7}
\end{equation}
where \(T_{\!\mathrm{corr},i}=(z_i-\mu_c)^{\!\top}J_i^{\!\top}e_c\) and
\(T_{k,i}=(z_i-\mu_c)^{\!\top}J_i^{\!\top}e_k\). (The full derivation is provided in Appendix~\ref{appC}.)

Rewrite $T_{\!\mathrm{corr},i}$ as $T_{\!\mathrm{corr},i}
  =\|z_i-\mu_c\|\,\bigl\|J_i^{\!\top}e_c\bigr\|\,
    \cos\theta_i $, with \(\theta_i\) the angle between \(z_i-\mu_c\) and \(J_i^{\!\top}e_c\).
Empirically, \(J_i^{\!\top}e_c\) points toward the direction that pulls a
sample toward its class centre, so \(0\le\theta_i<90^{\circ}\) for nearly
all training points; hence \(T_{\!\mathrm{corr},i}\ge0\).

Because one-hot supervision ensures \(p_{ic}\le1\), the factor \((p_{ic}-1)\)
satisfies \((p_{ic}-1)\le0\).
Therefore the product $(p_{ic}-1)\,T_{\!\mathrm{corr},i}\;\le\;0$, meaning the first term in Eq.~\ref{eq:7} is non-positive for every sample.  

