\subsubsection{Word Meaning Representation}
Although certain linguistic features appear critical for the model to solve the SSL objective, it remains unclear whether semantic content—specifically word meaning—is among them. To investigate this, \citet{pasad2021layer} assess the encoding of word meaning in wav2Vec 2.0 by computing the CCA similarity between word segment representations and GloVe embeddings \cite{pennington2014glove}, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:word_meaning_representation}. The results indicate that the middle layers—layers 7–8 in the Base model and 14–16 in the Large-60k model—encode the richest contextual information. Notably, the narrower plateau of peak performance in these curves compared to the MI curves in Figure \ref{fig:word_identity} suggests that central layers are more specialized in capturing semantic content, whereas peripheral layers primarily encode lower-level linguistic features without semantic abstraction.

