% \begin{abstract}
% Recent advances in human motion generation have led to dedicated models for either locomotion or isolated gesture synthesis, particularly in the context of co-speech gestures. However, situated gesturing—where communicative gestures are grounded in spatial references to the surrounding environment—remains underexplored. In human communication, referential expressions are deeply connected to the physical context, making spatial grounding a core component of truly situated interactions. In this paper, we address this gap by presenting a new multimodal dataset focused on referential communication in 3D environments. We combine two complementary resources: (1) a synthetic dataset of spatially grounded gestures for referential communication , and (2) MM-Conv, a VR-based dataset capturing two-party dialogues with spontaneous referential gestures. Together, these resources yield over 8 hours of synchronized motion, speech, gaze, and scene information. To facilitate future modeling efforts, we also convert the data into the HumanML3D-compatible format, aligning with emerging standards in indoor human behavior modeling. Our contribution lays the foundation for advancing research in situated gesture generation, bridging isolated gesture modeling and spatially aware human motion generation, and fostering more expressive, communicative virtual agents.
% \end{abstract}
% \begin{abstract} Recent advances in human motion generation have led to specialized models targeting either descriptive motion generation in 3D scenes—such as locomotion and object interaction—or isolated co-speech gesture synthesis, focusing on aligning gestures with the semantic content of speech. Descriptive motion generation typically emphasizes realistic navigation and environmental interactions, while co-speech gesture generation centers on producing expressive body movements synchronized with utterances, often without reference to the surrounding space.

% However, situated gesturing—where communicative gestures are grounded in spatial references to objects and locations in the environment—remains significantly underexplored. In natural human communication, referential expressions inherently tie language, gesture, and spatial context together, making spatial grounding crucial for truly situated interactions.

% In this paper, we address this gap by introducing a new multimodal dataset focused on referential communication in 3D environments. We combine two complementary resources: (1) a synthetic dataset of spatially grounded gestures for referential communication, and (2) MM-Conv, a VR-based dataset capturing two-party dialogues with spontaneous referential gestures. Together, these resources yield over 8 hours of synchronized motion, speech, gaze, and scene information. To facilitate future modeling efforts, we convert all data into the HumanML3D-compatible format, aligning with emerging standards in indoor human behavior modeling. Our contribution lays the foundation for advancing research in situated gesture generation, bridging the gap between isolated gesture modeling and spatially aware human motion generation, and fostering the development of more expressive and communicative virtual agents. \end{abstract}



