\subsection{Model Checking of the Product}
We further study the properties of the product. 
Interestingly, the behaviour of the product is somewhat similar to MCs, while they are not (sub)stochastic.
In this section, we fix initial states $x_0 \in X$ and $y_0\in Y$ of a given MC $c$ and MFA $d$, respectively. We assume that $d$ is \emph{trim}~\cite{WeberS91,AllauzenMR11}, that is, for any $y\in Y$, there is an accepting run from $y_0$ that visits $y$ along the run. 
% This assumption does not lose any generality because we focus on the model checking $q\big(\mu \Phi_c(x_0),  \mu \Phi_d(y_0)\big) $ from the initial states $x_0, y_0$. 
By~\cref{prop:correctmcmfas}, the solution of the model checking problem coincides with the semantics $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)$ of the product. 

First, we show a sufficient condition that ensures the finiteness of the semantics of the product. 
Our sufficient condition is indeed simple: It requires that the given MFA is \emph{polynomially ambiguous}~\cite{WeberS91}.

\begin{proposition}
    \label{prop:finiteSufficient}
    The semantics $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)\in \exreal$ is finite 
    if the trim MFA $d$ is polynomially ambiguous, that is, there is a constant $C$ and $n\in \nat$ suth that for any $w\in A^{+}$, the following inequality holds: $\mu \Phi_d(y_0)(w) \leq C\cdot |w|^n$, where $|w|$ is the length of $w$.
  \end{proposition}
\begin{proofs}
    The proof follows from the finiteness of the $n$-th termination moment (e.g.~\cite{WinklerK23}) for finite MCs. See~\cref{subsec:proofNtermination} for the details. 
\end{proofs}

The assumption of the polynomial ambiguity is essential: The semantics may diverge to $\infty$ if the MFA $d$ is not polynomially ambiguous. 
See~\cref{subsec:semanticsDiverge} for the details. 

To test the polynomial ambiguity of MFAs, we first translate MFAs into NFAs (with $\epsilon$-transitions) with preserving the number of accepting runs by adding new states for each weighted transition. 
Then we can run a polynomial-time algorithm that decides whether a given NFA is polynomially ambiguous~\cite{AllauzenMR11}.
See~\cref{subsec:proofPolAmb} for the proof. 
\begin{proposition}
  \label{prop:proofPolAmb}
  Given a trim MFA, deciding its polynomial ambiguity is solvable in polynomial-time if we fix the maximum weight that appears in its weighted transitions. 
\end{proposition}

Assume that the semantics $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)\in \exreal$ is finite. 
We then show that the semantics is a part of a unique solution of a certain equation systems, implying that the model checking problem is solvable in polynomial-time.
\begin{definition}[underlying directed graph, reachable]
    The \emph{underlying directed graph} $(V, E)$ of $c\otimes_{\lambda} d$ is given by $V \defeq X\times Y + \{\checkmark\}$ and 
    \[
    E\defeq \Big\{\big((x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2)\big) \ \Big | \  (c\otimes_{\lambda} d)(x_1, y_1)(x_2, y_2) > 0 \Big\} \bigcup  \Big\{\big((x, y), \checkmark\big) \ \Big | \  (c\otimes_{\lambda} d)(x, y)(\checkmark) > 0 \Big\}.
    \]
    We say that a state $(x, y)$ of the product is \emph{reachable} to a state $v\in V$ if $v$ is reachable from $(x, y)$ on the underlying directed graph $(V, E)$. 
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
  \label{lem:chreachability}
    Given a state $(x, y)\in X\times Y$, its semantics is positive $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x, y) > 0$ iff $(x, y)$ is reachable to $\checkmark$.
\end{lemma}
By~\cref{lem:chreachability}, we assume that $(x_0, y_0)$ is reachable to $\checkmark$.
Let $Z\subseteq X\times Y + \{\checkmark\}$ be the set of states that are reachable from $(x_0, y_0)$ on the underlying directed graph. 

% Similar to MCs, the semantics of products becomes the unique solution of a certain equation system. 
% This characterisation immediately yields a polynomial-time algorithm by solving the equation system. 

\begin{proposition}
    \label{prop:uniqueSolution}
    Assume that the semantics $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)\in \exreal$ is finite. 
    The semantics $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)$ is a part of the unique solution $(u_{z})_{z\in Z}$ of the following equation system. 
    \begin{align*}
        u_{z} &= 0 &&\text{ if $z$ is not reachable to $\checkmark$,}\\
        u_{z} &= 1 &&\text{ if $z = \checkmark$,}\\
        % u_{z} &= \big(c\otimes_{\lambda} d\big)(z)(\checkmark)\cdot u_{\checkmark}+ \sum_{z'\in X\times Y}\big(c\otimes_{\lambda} d\big)(z)(z') \cdot u_{z'} &&\text{ otherwise. }
        u_{z} &= \sum_{z'\in Z}\big(c\otimes_{\lambda} d\big)(z)(z') \cdot u_{z'} &&\text{ otherwise. }
    \end{align*} 

As a corollary, computing $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)$ is solvable in polynomial-time if all transition probabilities are rational, assuming that the semantics $\mu \Phi_{c\otimes_{\lambda} d}(x_0, y_0)\in \exreal$ is finite.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proofs}
    We follow the proof for the product of MCs and unambiguous B\"{u}chi automata~\cite{BaierK00023}, which is indeed easily applicable for our case with minor changes.  
