\section{Contribution}
\label{sec:contribution}
The main result we show in this paper is that 
if $(G,\mathcal{H},\mathcal{K})$ is a cross-free intersection system of genus $g$, then there
is an intersection support $\tilde{Q}$ of genus at most $g$. 
In order to show that $\tilde{Q}$ exists, we show that primal and dual supports of genus at most $g$ exist for
cross-free graph systems of genus $g$. These results are a generalization of the results of Raman and Ray~\cite{RR18}, 
who showed similar results for non-piercing regions in the plane. While some of the techniques in the two papers are
similar, several new ideas are required for the results to go through. Besides generalization to surfaces, 
our results have the advantage that they use basic graph operations and do not require delicate topological arguments
as in~\cite{RR18}. We also give applications of our results to several packing, covering problems and coloring problems.

\hide{
The problem of deciding if a hypergraph admits a support from a restricted family of graphs, 
and the problem of deciding whether a given family of hypergraphs always admits a support from a restricted graph class 
has received wide attention from different communities, starting with
the work of Johnson and Pollack~\cite{Johnson1987Hypergraph}, and Zykov~\cite{zykov1974hypergraphs}.
The motivation for our work however, comes from the analysis of a class of \emph{local-search algorithms} that
yield optimal approximation factors for many packing and covering problems
with geometric objects in the plane. 
Starting with the work of Chan and Har-Peled~\cite{ChanH12}, and Mustafa and Ray~\cite{mustafa2010improved},
the framework to analyze the performance of these local-search algorithms has been to show the existence of a 
suitable \emph{local-search graph} corresponding to the problem at hand~\cite{DBLP:conf/walcom/AschnerKMY13,BasuRoy2018,CabelloG14,GibsonP10,krohn2014guarding}.
Raman and Ray~\cite{RR18} showed that for many packing and covering problems involving geometric objects in the plane, 
there is a unified way to construct a support; and this support suffices for the analysis of the local-search
algorithms. In particular, the authors show that if the geometric regions $\mathcal{D}$ in the plane are 
\emph{non-piercing}, i.e., each $D\in\mathcal{D}$ is connected, 
and for any $D,D'\in\mathcal{D}$, $D\setminus D'$ and $D'\setminus D$
is connected, then there is a planar support graph corresponding to the packing/covering problems
with these geometric regions.

The result of Raman and Ray~\cite{RR18} and the previous results are restricted
to problems in the plane. Our goal is to go beyond the geometric and planar setting, and consider
hypergraphs defined on higher genus surfaces, and restricted hypergraphs that do not arise in geometric settings. 
To that end, we consider hypergraphs defined on a host graph. As stated in the introduction, this generalizes the planar setting.

We study mainly two settings: When the host graph has bounded genus, 
and when the host graph has bounded treewidth. Along the way, we also obtain results when the underlying host graph is
outerplanar.
While the results for bounded genus graphs roughly follow the proof outline of~\cite{RR18}, 
several new ideas are required for the proofs to go through. In particular, it turns out that
for graphs of bounded genus, the non-piercing condition is insufficient for the existence of sparse supports. 
We introduce the notion of \emph{cross-free} subgraphs and show that if $(G,\mathcal{H})$ is a cross-free
system and $G$ has genus $g$, then there exist primal, dual, and intersection supports of genus at most $g$. 
Further, if $(G,\mathcal{H},\mathcal{K})$ is a cross-free system of genus $g$, then there exists an intersection support of genus at most $g$. These results
directly generalize the results of~\cite{RR18}, and have the advantage of using basic graph operations, and not
requiring delicate topological arguments.
}
\subsection{Existence of Supports}

We obtain the following results for the construction of primal, dual, and intersection supports for cross-free systems.

\begin{restatable}{thm}{primalsupport}
\label{thm:primalsupport}
Let $(G,\mathcal{H})$ be a cross-free graph system of genus $g$.
For any coloring $c:V(G)\to\{\R,\B\}$, there exists a primal support $Q$ of genus at most $g$ on $\B(V)$.
If a cross-free embedding of $G$ with respect to $\mathcal{H}$ is given, 
then $Q$ can be constructed in 
$O(mn^5)$ time where $m=|\HH|$, and $n=|V(G)|$.
\end{restatable}

\begin{restatable}{thm}{dualembedded}
\label{thm:dualembedded}
