We start by showing that if no red vertex is
maximal and the subgraphs in $\mathcal{H}$ are connected (and not necessarily cross-free), then it is easy to construct a 
support.

Our construction of a support in the general case
follows by repeated application of the Vertex Bypassing operation
to a maximal red vertex. We show that each Vertex Bypassing operation 
decreases the number of maximal red vertices, and hence we
eventually obtain a system with no maximal red vertices. While the lemma below
is stated for bounded genus graphs, it works for any herediary family closed
under edge-contractions.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:easypsupport2}
Let $(G,\mathcal{H})$ be a graph system of genus $g$ such that each $H\in\mathcal{H}$ is a connected subgraph of $G$.
Then, for any coloring $c:V(G)\to\{\R,\B\}$
s.t. no vertex in $\R(V(G))$ is maximal,
there is a primal support
$Q$ on $\B(V(G))$ of genus at most $g$. Furthermore,
$Q$ can be constructed in $O(|V(G)|^2|\mathcal{H}|)$ time.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We prove by induction on $|\R(V(G))|$. If $|\R(V(G))|=0$, then $Q=G$ is the desired support.
Suppose the statement holds for any graph system 
satisfying the conditions in the lemma
with less than $k$ red vertices.

Let $(G,\mathcal{H})$ be a graph system of genus $g$ such that each $H\in\mathcal{H}$ induces a connected subgraph of $G$,
and $c:V(G)\to\{\R,\B\}$ s.t. no vertex in $\R(V(G))$ is maximal,
and $|\R(V(G))|=k$.

Consider an arbitrary vertex $u\in\R(V(G))$. 
Since $u$ is not maximal, by definition, 
there is an edge $e=\{u,v\}\in E(G)$ such that 
$\mathcal{H}_e =\mathcal{H}_u$. 
Since $\mathcal{H}_e\subseteq\mathcal{H}_v$, it follows
that $\mathcal{H}_u\subseteq\mathcal{H}_v$. 
Let $G'=G/\{u,v\}$, i.e., the graph obtained by contracting
the edge $\{u,v\}$ with the new vertex taking the color
$c(v)$.
Note that $G'$ has genus at most $g$ since contracting an edge in $G$ 
preserves the embedding on the same surface as $G$ does. 
Let $\mathcal{H}'$ denote the
subgraphs in $G'$ corresponding to $\mathcal{H}$.
Each subgraph in $\mathcal{H}'$ clearly induces a connected subgraph of $G'$, and $|\R(V(G'))|=k-1$.
By the inductive hypothesis, there is a support $Q$ for $(G',\mathcal{H}')$.
Note that $Q$ is also a support for $(G,\mathcal{H})$ 
since the set of blue vertices in $H$ remains unchanged in $(G',\mathcal{H}')$ for each $H\in\mathcal{H}$. 

Each edge contraction can be performed in time $O(|V(G)||\mathcal{H}|)$ (assuming $G$ is represented as an adjacency list) and
the number of edge contractions is bounded above by $|\R(V(G))|\le|V(G)|$, it follows that
a primal support for $(G,\mathcal{H})$ can be computed in 
time $O(|V(G)|^2|\mathcal{H}|)$.
\end{proof}

