\subsubsection{Sub-exponential time algorithms}
In this subsection, we show a sub-exponential time algorithm for the \textbf{Generalized Packing Problem}.

\subexp*
\begin{proof}
Let $I=(\HH,E)$ be the graph constructed on $\HH$, where $H,H'\in\HH$ are adjacent in $I$ if and only if there is a $K\in\KK$ such that $\KK\cap H\ne\emptyset$ and $K\cap H'\ne\emptyset$.
By Theorem \ref{thm:intsupport}, $(G,\HH,\KK)$ admits an intersection support $\tilde{Q}$ of genus at most $g$. Since $\tilde{Q}$ has bounded genus,
Theorem \ref{thm:gilbert} implies that it admits a separator $S$ of size $O(\sqrt{gn})$.
Let $A$ and $B$ be two separated sets of vertices in $\tilde{Q}\setminus S$, each containing at most $n/2$ vertices.

The algorithm is as follows: For each subset $S'\subseteq S$,
we add $S'$ into our solution, remove the neighbors of $S'$ in $A$ and $B$,
and recurse on the two sub-problems $A\setminus N_I(S')$ and $B\setminus N_I(S')$, where $N_I(S')$ are the set of all neighbors of the vertices
in $S'$ in the graph $I$, i.e., $N_I(S')=\{H'\in I: H\sim H',\mbox{ for some } H\in S'\}$.
Since $S$ is a balanced separator, $|A\setminus N_I(S')|$ and $|B\setminus N_I(S')|$ are both at most $n/2$. It is clear that the solution obtained is
a packing, and the running time of the algorithm is given by
the recurrence $T(n)=2T(n/2)+2^{O(\sqrt{gn})}$, which is $2^{O(\sqrt{gn})}$.

To show that the algorithm returns an optimal solution, fix an optimal
solution $\OO$, and suppose the algorithm returns a maximum size packing
for graphs with fewer than $n$ vertices. 
Since we enumerate over all subsets of $S$,
in some iteration, we have $S'=S\cap\OO$. By the inductive hypothesis,
the packing obtained from $A\setminus N_I(S')$ and $B\setminus N_I(S')$ are
both at least $\OO\cap (A\setminus S')$ and $\OO\cap (B\setminus S')$,
and hence the solution returned is at least as large as $\OO$.
\end{proof}

