Let $\mathcal{R}$ be a collection of weakly
non-piercing regions on a surface of genus $g$.
Consider the boundaries $\partial R$ and $\partial R'$ of two regions
$R$ and $R'$ in $\mathcal{R}$, respectively. Then, $\partial R\cap\partial R'$ consists of a finite collection of
isolated points and arcs. If at an isolated point, the boundaries of the regions cross, then we call this point
a \emph{crossing point}. Otherwise, we call it a \emph{touching point}. Equivalently, a point $p\in\partial R\cap \partial R'$ is a touching point if in an arbitrarily small ball $B$ around $p$, the boundaries $\partial R$ and
$\partial R'$ can be modified by an arbitrarily small amount so that $\partial R$ and $\partial R'$ are disjoint in $B$.
