Let $\HH'$ and $\KK'$ denote respectively, the families of connected subgraphs of $G$ induced by the regions in $\HH$ and $\KK$.
Clearly, $\HH$ is in bijection with $\HH'$, and $\KK$ is in bijection with $\KK'$.
Moreover, by construction, any two regions $R_1,R_2\in\HH\cup\KK$ intersect if and only if the corresponding subgraphs $R'_1,R'_2\in\HH'\cup\KK'$ share a vertex in $G$.
Hence the underlying intersection hypergraph remains the same in the two settings.

We claim that $(G,\HH',\KK')$ is cross-free.
If possible, suppose $(G,\HH')$ is not cross-free.
Note that for any two regions $H_i,H_j\in\HH$, if $H_i\setminus H_j$ is connected, then the corresponding induced subgraph $H_i'\setminus H_j'$ is connected.
Since $(G,\HH)$ is not cross-free, there are two subgraphs $H_i',H_j'\in\HH'$ 
corresponding to regions $H_i$ and $H_j$ in $\mathcal{H}$ respectively such that in the reduced
graph $R(H_i',H_j')$ (see Definition~\ref{def:reducedgraph})), there is a vertex $v\in H_i'\cap H_j'$ that is a crossing vertex.
Then there are four edges in cyclic order around $v$: $(\{v, v_1\}, \{v, v_2\}, \{v, v_3\}, \{v,v_4\})$ so that
$v_1, v_3\in H_i'\setminus H_j'$ and $v_2, v_4\in H_j'\setminus H_i'$. 
Assume wlog that $H_i'\setminus H_j'$ induces a connected subgraph since the regions in $\HH$ are weakly non-piercing.
Hence, there is a path $P$ in $G$ from $v_1$ to $v_3$ that lies entirely in $H_i'\setminus H_j'$. 
Adding $v$ and the edges $\{v,v_1\}$ and
$\{v,v_3\}$ to $P$, we obtain a cycle $C$ such that all vertices that lie on $C$, or in its interior,
lie on or in the interior of $C_{H_i}$.
Hence, all vertices of $G$
that lie in the interior of $C$ also belong to $H_i'$.
By our assumption of crossing, one of $v_2$ or $v_4$ lies in the interior of $C$ and the other lies outside $C$.
Assume the former. But this implies that $v_2\in H_i'$, a contradiction.
Hence, $(G,\HH')$ is cross-free.
By the same reason, $(G,\KK')$ is also cross-free.
}
\end{proof}


