\begin{proof}
We first show how can use the existence of primal/dual/intersection supports for cross-free graph systems to
obtain primal/dual/intersection supports for weakly non-piercing regions.

By Lemma~\ref{lem:touchcross}, the dual arrangement graph $G$ defined by the regions $\mathcal{D}$ is cross-free.
For each cell in the arrangement that contains a point of $P$, we color the corresponding vertex in $G$ blue, otherwise,
we color it red. 
For each boundary arc or touching point between adjacent cells in the arrangement of $\mathcal{D}$
that contains a point $p\in P$, we do the following: 
Let $e=\{u,v\}$ denote the edge between the vertices
$u$ and $v$ corresponding to adjacent cells $C_u$ and $C_v$. We subdivide $e$ with a blue point $p$.
We then extend the subgraphs corresponding to the regions containing $C_u$ or $C_v$ to also contain $p$. 
The resulting graph
remains cross-free, as the difference of any pair of subgraphs remains unchanged. Let $\B(V)$ denote the set of blue vertices
of $G$.
By Theorem~\ref{thm:primalsupport}, there is a primal support $Q$ on $\B(V)$ for $(G,\mathcal{D})$. Each vertex $v\in \B(V)$ corresponds to a
set $P_v$ of vertices of $P$ that lie in the cell $C_v$ corresponding to $v$, or $v$ represents the boundary
between two cells, and $P_v$ are the points of $P$ that lie on this boundary. To obtain a primal support for the given hypergraph $(P,\mathcal{D})$, we choose
a representative point $v$ of $P_v$ for each $v\in Q$, and connect the remaining points of $P_v$ to it so that
they are degree 1 vertices. We thus obtain a primal support for the arrangement $(P,\mathcal{D})$ that is planar.

In order to obtain a dual support fro $(P,\mathcal{D})$, we start with the dual arrangement graph $G$. Let $e=\{u,v\}$ be an edge in $G$,
and let $C_u$ and $C_v$ denote the corresponding adjacent cells in the arrangement $\mathcal{D}$. Suppose $C_u$ and $C_v$
have a vertex of $P$ on their boundary, then we subdivide $e$ with a vertex $v$, and for each subgraph corresponding
to a region containing $C_u$ or $C_v$, we extend it to also contain $v$. As in the previous argument, the resulting
graph remains cross-free. Abusing notation slightly, we use $G$ to also denote this modified graph. Let 
$(G,\mathcal{D}')$ denote the cross-free graph system. By Theorem~\ref{thm:dualembedded}, there is a dual support
$Q^*$ on $\mathcal{D}'$ and this corresponds to a dual support on $\mathcal{D}$. 

By the results in~\cite{RR18}, since there is a primal support for $(P,\mathcal{D})$, it implies that the
Point Packing problem admits a PTAS. Further, by the results of Basu Roy~\cite{BasuRoy2018}, it follows that
Set Packing with constant capacities admits a PTAS. A dual support for $(P,\mathcal{D})$ implies that the
Set Packing problem admits a PTAS. The fact that the Set Packing problem with bounded capacities admits a
PTAS follows by the results in Basu Roy~\cite{BasuRoy2018}.

For the intersection support, we proceed along the lines of the dual support. Let $\mathcal{H}$ denote
the subgraphs in the dual arrangement graph $G$. The subgraphs $\mathcal{K}$ are obtained by making a copy
of each $H\in\mathcal{H}$. The intersection system $(G,\mathcal{H},\mathcal{K})$ admits a planar intersection
support by~\ref{thm:intsupport}. Now, by the results in~\cite{RR18}, it follows that the dominating set problem
in the intersection graph of weakly non-piercing regions admits a PTAS.
\end{proof}
}

