\section{Introduction}
Reliable ego-motion estimation is fundamental to autonomous robotic platforms. Early solutions rely on GNSS/INS, while more recent SLAM-based methods integrate diverse sensors—such as cameras, LiDARs, and radars—making them more adaptable and broadly applicable.
%satellite-based localization approaches，such as the  and the  based on various onboard sensors.
%Comparing to the , Using onboard sensors, such as cameras, Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR), and intertial measurement units (IMUs), and combinations of them, are favored in many senarios as these sensors do not depend on external signals.
Successful deployments of SLAM-based approaches on various platforms utilize combinations of sensors such as cameras, LiDARs and IMUs leveraging their measurements to fully resolve all degrees of freedom in platform pose estimation.
Nevertheless, robot systems with agile maneuverability, such as aerobatic UAVs and racing UGVs, often require velocity state feedback to achieve aggressive motion control, and the primary sensors mentioned above fail to capture instantaneous velocity of those platforms in highly dynamic scenarios. 

