% \textbf{Linear Velocity}:
% Linear velocity measurements can be derived from the IMU's linear acceleration and the radar's Doppler velocity in the dataset. The Pure-IMU method solely integrates the IMU's acceleration, which leads to significant accumulation of velocity errors across all sequences. Both River and RIO leverage a fusion of IMU acceleration and radar Doppler measurements, providing access to both internal motion cues and external observations. However, RIO introduces erroneous radar scan matching during the process, which severely degrades velocity estimation accuracy.
% River, in contrast, constructs a velocity space, enabling effective smoothing of noise in velocity estimation and yielding better performance overall. The remaining errors are mainly attributed to flight-induced or radar-induced vibrations, particularly along the radar’s Z-axis.

\textbf{Linear Velocity}:
Linear velocity is obtained from IMU acceleration and radar Doppler data. Pure-IMU relies only on IMU acceleration, causing large velocity errors in all sequences. River and RIO fuse IMU and radar data, combining internal and external motion cues. However, RIO’s radar scan matching introduces errors that hurt accuracy. River’s velocity space smoothing reduces noise, achieving better results. Remaining errors mainly come from vibrations during flight or radar, especially along the radar’s Z-axis.

% Our proposed method, Twist-Estimator, which estimates linear velocity using only radar Doppler measurements via a least-squares approach. Due to the noise level of Doppler velocity, conventional RANSAC-based outlier rejection becomes ineffective. Instead, we adopt a statistical filtering strategy to remove outliers from the Doppler point cloud. Experimental results show that our method achieves the best performance in several sequences, such as dji2, dji4, and dji5. Even in the absence of high-frequency, accurate linear motion signals from the IMU, the proposed approach still achieves second-best performance in the remaining sequences.
Our method, Twist-Estimator, estimates linear velocity using only radar Doppler data with a least-squares approach. Experiments show top performance in sequences like $\mathtt{dji}2$, $\mathtt{dji}4$, and $\mathtt{dji}5$, and second-best results in others, even without high-frequency IMU data.
% \vspace{-0.5mm}
Twist-Estimator and River form a strong comparison. River benefits from high-frequency IMU updates and performs better in sequences with stable motion. However, when the motion becomes highly dynamic or the radar suffers from strong vibrations, its performance degrades rapidly, while our method remains more robust under such conditions. \\
\textbf{Angular Velocity}:
Pure-IMU, River, and RIO directly utilize gyroscope measurements from the IMU. Pure-IMU lacks an optimization framework; thus, its angular velocity error primarily reflects the measurement noise level. RIO adopts an EKF framework, enabling joint estimation of angular velocity and constraint errors. As a result, it achieves optimal performance in certain sequences, such as $\mathtt{dji}1$, $\mathtt{dji}2$, $\mathtt{dji}3$, and $\mathtt{dji}10$, while in others, performance varies significantly due to motion-induced challenges.
River models trajectories using continuous splines on the $\mathbb{SO}(3)$ manifold, closely approximating the nonlinear characteristics of rotation. This allows it to effectively smooth angular velocity noise, yielding consistently optimal or suboptimal results across most sequences.

% Both CMax-SLAM and the proposed Twist-Estimator rely on sparse event streams. CMax-SLAM requires a high event density for image reconstruction and motion capture, excelling in structured, high-contrast environments but often failing in open or low-texture scenes where it assumes zero velocity. In contrast, Twist-Estimator estimates angular velocity via normal-flow optical flow with local inlier filtering to address the aperture problem, and uses radar-based linear velocity compensation to approximate metric scale. This radar-event fusion allows Twist-Estimator to better counteract IMU noise under intense motion, achieving superior angular velocity estimation in challenging sequences (e.g., dji7, dji9). However, its accuracy and update rate are limited by radar noise and low frequency. Overall, Twist-Estimator outperforms CMax-SLAM in semi-open scenes (dji1-3, dji7-10), while CMax-SLAM performs better in building scenes (dji4-6). Compared to direct IMU angular velocity measurements, both methods slightly underperform in most sequences.\\
Both CMax-SLAM and Twist-Estimator use sparse event streams. CMax-SLAM needs high event density and works well in structured, high-contrast scenes but struggles in open or low-texture areas, often assuming zero velocity. Twist-Estimator estimates angular velocity via normal-flow optical flow with local filtering and compensates linear velocity using radar data for metric scale. This fusion helps it handle IMU noise better during intense motion, outperforming CMax-SLAM in challenging sequences (e.g., $\mathtt{dji7, dji9}$). However, its accuracy and update rate are limited by radar noise and frequency. Overall, Twist-Estimator excels in semi-open scenes ($\mathtt{dji1-3}$, $\mathtt{dji7-10}$), while CMax-SLAM is better in building scenes ($\mathtt{dji4-6}$). Both slightly underperform compared to direct IMU angular velocity measurements.\\
