Most of the research in AoI considers the case in which the age of the packets to be delivered is zero, which corresponds to packets entering the communication channel as soon as they are generated. Under this assumption, a recent but already large body of literature has analyzed for example the average AoI \cite{kaul_real:2012}, optimal scheduling \cite{bedewy:2019}, game theoretic aspects \cite{nguyen:2018}, energy harvesting \cite{arafa:2019}, and other specific metrics such as peak AoI \cite{costa:2016}, Age of Incorrect of Information \cite{maatouk:2020} and Query of AoI \cite{chiariotti:2022}. The book \cite{sun:2020} provides a comprehensive survey on the topic, and a repository of papers on AoI is maintained at \cite{AoIRepository}.
%

In this paper, the focus is on cases where packets to be delivered have a non-zero age. This situation arises when packets have already spent time in another system before reaching the final link, which is common in multi-hop networks, distributed sensing, and edge computing. For instance, in a wireless network, updates may be generated at a sensor, relayed through multiple intermediate nodes, and eventually delivered to a destination. By the time they enter a specific queue, they have already accumulated some age from prior hops. Another example is found in a sensor network, in which sensors collect and locally process data before transmitting it to a remote server. In such cases, the AoI at the receiver reflects both the local processing time and the transmission delays. Figure \ref{fig::motivation_example}(a) depicts an elementary example of this model with some potential application scenarios. Between the packet's generation time and its arrival at the system queue, some delay exists, which may be due to various causes, such as intermediate buffers (b.1), network latency or preprocessing (b.2), or sensor remoteness in a multi-hop network (b.3). This delay produces a sequence of initial ages on the incoming packets.
%

The main result provides an expression for the Average Age of Information (AAoI) when packets have a non-zero initial age ($\Delta^A$), relating it directly to the AAoI in the standard case where packets arrive with zero age ($\Delta^0$). Specifically, it is proven that $\Delta^A=\Delta^0+\lambda^e\E{Y_n A_{n-1}},$ where $\lambda^e$ is the effective arrival rate of packets, and $A_{n-1}$ and $Y_n$ refer to the sequences of packet arrival ages and interdeparture times, respectively. In the case the age process and inter-departure times are statistically independent (which, as shown in Section~\ref{sec::applications}, may occur in various scenarios), the equation reduces to $\Delta^A=\Delta^0+\E{A_{n-1}}.$ For more complex systems where the dependency structure is unknown or cannot be studied analytically, specific bounds can be established to determine the range within which the final value of $\Delta^A$ lies. The applicability of the main result is illustrated in various settings, including a forwarding queue, tandem queues and retrial queues. Additionally, the validity of interval estimation for $\Delta^A$ is tested in some of these scenarios, and its practical usefulness is highlighted in an application to a multi-queued tandem. 
%

The rest of the paper is organised as follows. In Section \ref{sec::model}, the system model is introduced and the age process with aged updates is characterised. In Section \ref{sec::result}, the main result of this article is presented and demonstrated. In Section \ref{sec::applications}, some illustrative applications of the model are described. Finally, some final comments are given in Section \ref{sec::conclusions} together with some suggestions for future research.
