%
\section{Model Description}
%
%
\subsection{System model}\label{sec::model}
%
%
Consider a communication system through which a destination node receives updates about a process of interest. At epochs $g_n$, information updates are generated at the source and transmitted to the destination as packets. However, unlike standard models, we assume that updates do not enter the system immediately upon generation. Instead, they arrive at the queueing system after incurring a certain delay.
%

Packets enter the queueing system at epochs $t_n$ according to an arrival process of rate $\lambda$ with i.i.d. inter-arrival times $X_n = t_n - t_{n-1}$. Each arriving packet has a corresponding initial age $A_n \geq 0$, defined as the time elapsed between the packet’s generation and its arrival at the system. The initial ages $\left\{A_n\right\}$ are modelled as a stationary and ergodic sequence of identically distributed, but not necessarily independent, variables. Importantly, we allow $A_n$ to statistically depend on the inter-arrival times $X_n$ and the inter-departure times $Y_n$. This is a key aspect that introduces additional complexity in the model. Still, it also reflects a wide variety of realistic scenarios, enhancing the usefulness and relevance of this framework. The stationarity assumption on $A_n$ ensures that the process remains bounded in expectation and avoids pathological cases of ever-increasing update ages. 
%

Upon arrival, a packet is either discarded, queued for future processing, or served immediately, depending on the queue size, the packet management policy, and the system's state. Packets that are not discarded enter the system and complete the service. These are referred to as \emph{effective arrivals}, and the effective inter-arrival times are denoted by $X_n^e$. Once a packet enters the server, it stays in service for a $\mu$-rate exponential time $S_n\sim\expo{\mu}$, independent of the inter-arrival time. The service completion times are denoted by $t'_n$, and define the sequence of departures. The \emph{system time} of packet $n$ is $T_n=t'_n-t_n$, and the \emph{inter-departure time} is $Y_n=t'_n-t'_{n-1}$. 
%

The timeliness of information at the destination node can be quantified using the metric AoI, which is discussed in the next section.
%
%
\subsection{Age of Information with aged updates}
%
%

Define the counting process of delivered packets 
\begin{equation}\label{def::counting_process}
	N(s)=\max\left\{n: t'_n\leq s\right\}.
\end{equation} 
%

At any given time $s$, the timestamp of the last packet received at the destination is $g_{N(s)}$. The AoI is thus defined as the random process
\begin{equation}\label{def::age_def}
	\Delta(s) = s - g_{N(s)},\ s>0.
\end{equation}

%
\begin{figure}[htpb]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=0.54\textwidth]{img/aoi_aged_mm1_new.png}
	\caption{Example of age profile in a model with aged updates. The packet arriving at epoch $t_3$ carries a high age. When the packet is delivered at $t'_3$, it produces an upper jump on the age at the monitor. Disjoint areas $H_n$ are used to calculate the Average Age of Information.}\label{fig::aoi_path}
\end{figure}
%

A graphical illustration of the AoI evolution in this model is shown in Figure \ref{fig::aoi_path}. The process evolves piecewise linearly, with occasional jumps at delivery times $t'_n$. While it still maintains the typical sawtooth shape characteristic of such functions, some updates may enter the system with a high initial age. At the time of delivery of these updates, the age at the destination may increase instead of resetting to a lower value. This behaviour is a distinctive feature of models with aged updates and is not seen in standard AoI models with zero-aged updates \cite{yates:2021}.
%
Specifically, at times $t'_n$, the AoI resets from the peak value $A_{n-1}+X_n+T_n$ to $A_n+T_n$. Thus, if $A_n$ exceeds the threshold $X_n+A_{n-1}$, the delivery of this packet leads to an upper jump in the AoI function, as illustrated by packet 3 in Figure \ref{fig::aoi_path}. We term these ''far updates'' due to their similarity to distant status updates in asynchronous monitoring. 
