\subsubsection{Discovering Key Latent Directions Affecting Generation Metrics}

To visualize the main axes of topological structure variation across the microcircuits, we applied non-linear dimensionality reduction using t-SNE and identified topological features exhibiting gradients in the resulting embedding space (Figure~\ref{fig:tsne}). For each graph descriptor, we then aimed to find a consistent direction in the latent space that correlates with changes in its value. To achieve this, we divided the 500 test graphs into 20 value bins, each containing 5\% of the data, and trained a linear regression model to predict the bin index from the 32-dimensional latent vector. The constant gradient direction of the linear regression model is advantageous for identifying this direction~\cite{linear_reg}, and further details of this method are in Appendix~\ref{sec:linear_regression}. We plot the $\text{R}^2$ scores of these linear regression models in Figure~\ref{fig:correlations}(a). 

Since there might be some inherent correlation between the metrics, which results in similar or divergent gradient directions for different metrics, we computed the correlation between each pair of metrics directly from the test dataset, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:correlations}(b). We also computed the cosine similarity between each pair of gradient directions, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:correlations}(c). The relative magnitude of the cosine similarity between the correlation values and the gradient directions is largely consistent, further confirming that the gradient direction does indeed correspond to specific metrics. Further details regarding the correlations between different metrics are provided in Appendix\ref{sec:correlations_details}.

To validate whether these identified directions truly reflect changes in the corresponding metrics, we performed a traversal experiment. Starting from the mean latent vector of the test set, we moved along each metric's identified gradient direction and decoded the resulting latent vectors to observe the change in the metric's value. The details are shown in Appendix~\ref{sec:gradient_direction_moving}.


