A significant portion of our work in this section involves proving $\LS_+$-rank upper bounds for vertex-transitive graphs up to a certain size, which is largely aided by the fact that vertex-transitive graphs on up to $47$ vertices have been exhaustively listed (see~\cite{McKayR90, Skiena, RoyleH20}). 

We now determine $\overline{n}_+(2)$.

\begin{proposition}\label{propVT2}
$\overline{n}_+(2) = 8$. Moreover, the only vertex-transitive graph $G$ where $|V(G)| \leq 8$ and $r_+(G) = 2$ is $G_{\ref{figVertexTransitive234},1} \ce C_8^{\set{1,2}}$ (Figure~\ref{figVertexTransitive234}, left).
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $G$ is a $k$-regular vertex-transitive graph on $n$ vertices, and $r_+(G) =2$. To find the smallest such graph, we may assume that $G$ is connected (otherwise $G$ contains a proper subgraph with the same $\LS_+$-rank). From Lemma~\ref{lemnkl}, we know that $3 \leq k \leq n-4$, and so $n \geq 7$. If $n=7$, then $k=3$, and no such graph exists. If $n=8$, then $3 \leq k \leq 4$. There are a total of 5 such graphs~\cite{McKayR90, Skiena}, as listed in Figure~\ref{figVT2}.

