\subsubsection{Existence of representations}
A conservative minority tree $\mathcal{T}$ 
for which all local algebras $\mathbf{C}_v$ are simple (or projection minority) is called a \emph{simple} (or \emph{projection}, respectively) conservative minority tree. Note that, because every projection minority algebra $\mathbf{P}_n$ is simple, every projection conservative minority tree is a simple conservative minority tree. 

We establish in this section that every conservative minority algebra can be represented as the leaf algebra of a simple reduced conservative minority tree (Theorem~\ref{thm:representwithtreealgebras}). 
The following lemmas will allow us to use the concept of block congruences to obtain such representations (see Figure~\ref{fig:treesandpotatoes}).

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:congruencesoftreealgebras}
Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a conservative minority tree and let $\fA_\mathcal{T}$ be its leaf algebra. For every vertex $v$ of $\mathcal{T}$, the equivalence relation 
\[
\sim_v \; = \{(a,b): \text{$a=b$ or $a,b \leq_\mathcal{T} v$ }\} \subseteq L_\mathcal{T}^2
\]
is a block congruence of $\fA_\mathcal{T}$. Moreover, if 
$\mathbf{C}_v$ is for every $v \in T \setminus L_{\bT}$ a simple algebra, then every block congruence of $\mathbf{A}_\mathcal{T}$ is equal to a congruence of the form $\sim_v$ for some vertex $v$ of $\mathcal{T}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
First, it is clear that $\sim_v$ is an equivalence relation. Indeed, if there are distinct $a,b,c \in L_\mathcal{T}$ with $(a,b), (a,c) \in \; \sim_v$, then it is immediate that $(a,c) \in \; \sim_v$. Let us show that $\sim_v$ is preserved by $m$. To do so, it suffices to show that all \emph{basic translations} of $m_\mathcal{T}$ preserve $\sim_v$, i.e., those unary functions obtained by evaluating two coordinates at constants.
%\todo[inline]{Add reference to prelims} 
Take $a,b,c,d \in L_{\bT}$
%B_\mathbf{T}$\todo{What is $B_\mathbf{T}$?} 
so that $(a,b) \in \; \sim_v$. We argue that $(m_{\bT}(a,c,d), m_{\bT}(b,c,d)) \in \; \sim_v$. If $a=b$, there is nothing to prove, so we assume $a\neq b$. There are two cases to consider. 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Suppose that $c \leq v$ and $d \leq v$. It follows that $m_\mathcal{T}(a,c,d) \leq v$ and $m_\mathcal{T}(b,c,d) \leq v$, hence $(m_{\bT}(a,c,d), m_{\bT}(b,c,d)) \in \; \sim_v$.
    \item Suppose that at least one of $c$ or $d$ does not have $v$ as an upper bound. It follows that $\bigvee \{a,c,d\} = \bigvee \{b,c,d\} = \bigvee \{v,c,d\} =: w$. We have that $v \leq w$ and $v \neq w$, so $a_{w^\rightarrow} = b_{w^\rightarrow}$. In this case we have 
    \[
    m_{w}(a_{w^\rightarrow}, c_{w^\rightarrow}, d_{w^\rightarrow}) = m_{w}(b_{w^\rightarrow}, c_{w^\rightarrow}, d_{w^\rightarrow}),
    \]
    hence $(m_\mathcal{T}(a,c,d), m_\mathcal{T}(b,c,d)) \in \{(a,b),(c,c),(d,d)\} \subseteq \; \sim_v$.
\end{itemize}

The reasoning we just gave does not depend on the coordinates of $m_{\bT}$ that are evaluated at constants, so we have shown that all basic translations of $m_{\bT}$ preserve $\sim_v$. It follows that $m_{\bT}$ preserves $\sim_v$. 

Now suppose that $\mathbf{C}_v$ is for every $v \in T \setminus L_{\bT}$ a simple algebra. Let $\theta$ be a block congruence of $\fA_{\bT}$. Notice that the equality relation is of the form  $\sim_l$ for some leaf $l \in L_{\bT}$. Hence, we assume that $\theta$ is not the equality relation and so there is a nontrivial class $[\theta]_a$ for some leaf $a \in L_{\bT}$. Let $v = \bigvee [\theta]_a$ be the least upper bound of the set $[\theta]_a$ in $\mathcal{T}$. Clearly, $\theta \subseteq \; \sim_v$. We argue that $\theta = \; \sim_v$. First, notice that the domain of $\mathbf{C}_{v}$ has at least two elements, for if it had only one, say $w$, then $w \neq v$ and is also an upper bound of $[\theta]_a$. Next, notice that the set 
\[
X = \{s \in C_{v}: \text{ there exists $b \in [\theta]_a$ with $b \leq_\mathcal{T}s$} \}
\]
is a nontrivial block of a congruence of $\mathbf{C}_v$, hence $X = C_{v}$. This follows because we assume that $\mathbf{C}_{v}$ is simple and $X$ must have at least two elements as otherwise $v$ is not the least upper bound of $[\theta]_a$.

To see that $\theta= \; \sim_p$, take $c \in L_{\bT}$ with $c \leq_\mathcal{T} v$. We will show that $c$ is $\theta$-related to a branch that belongs to $[\theta]_a$. By our earlier observations, there exist $d,e \in [\theta]_a$ with $d_{v^\rightarrow} = c_{v^\rightarrow}$ and $e_{v^\rightarrow} \neq c_{v^\rightarrow}$. Because $\theta$ is a congruence, we have that $(m_\mathcal{T}(c,d,d), m_\mathcal{T}(c,d,e)) \in \theta$. The first entry of this pair is obviously equal to $c$. For the second, we consult the algebra $\mathbf{C}_{v}$ and compute
\[
m_{v}(c_{v^\rightarrow}, d_{v^\rightarrow}, e_{v^\rightarrow}) = m_{v}(c_{v^\rightarrow}, c_{v^\rightarrow}, e_{v^\rightarrow}) = e_{v^\rightarrow},
\]
hence $m_\mathcal{T}(c,d,e) = e$. Therefore, $(c,e) \in \theta$ and the proof is finished. 
\end{proof}



\begin{theorem}\label{thm:representwithtreealgebras}
Let $\fA = (A;m)$ be a conservative minority algebra. Then there exists a simple reduced conservative minority tree $\mathcal{T}$ such that $\fA$ is isomorphic to $\fA_\mathcal{T}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $T = \{[a]_\theta: \text{$a \in A$ and $\theta$ is a block congruence of $\fA$}
\}$. It follows from Proposition \ref{prop:blockintersection} that $T$ ordered by set inclusion is a tree. Indeed, the equality relation is a block congruence with no nontrivial classes, hence the minimal elements of $T$ are exactly the singleton subsets of $A$. The full relation is also a block congruence, with a single nontrivial class equal to $A$. Proposition \ref{prop:blockintersection} indicates that two classes of a block congruence must either have empty intersection or be comparable in the subset order, so there is a unique path %subset inclusion ordered path 
in $T$ from a given singleton class $\{a\} \in T$ to the root $A \in T$. 

Now we define the local conservative minority structure for $T$. Let $[a]_\theta \in T\setminus L_\mathcal{T}$. Since $\fA$ is a conservative minority algebra under the operation $m$, it follows that $[a]_\theta$ is also a conservative minority algebra under the restriction of $m$. By Corollary \ref{corollary:maximalcongruence}, there exists a unique maximal proper congruence on this algebra which we call $\lambda$. The set of children of $[a]_\theta$ in $T$ is equal to the set of equivalence classes of $\lambda$, that is, 
\[
C_{[a]_\theta} = \{[b]_\lambda : b \in [a]_\theta \}.
\]
To see this, notice that Proposition \ref{prop:blocksinsideblocks} guarantees that $\lambda$ extends to a congruence $\overline{\lambda} = \lambda \cup \{(c,c) : c \in A \}$ of $\fA$. Hence, all equivalence classes of $\lambda$ that are subsets of $[a]_\theta$ are also equivalence classes of a congruence on $\fA$ and therefore belong to $T$. So, all equivalance classes of $\lambda$ are below $[a]_\theta$ in the subset order on $T$ and the maximality of $\lambda$ therefore guarantees that the $C_{[a]_\theta}$ is equal to the set of equivalence classes of $\lambda$. Now we set the algebra $\mathbf{C}_{[a]_\theta}$ to be the quotient algebra with underlying set $([a]_\theta)/\lambda$. Since $\lambda$ is maximal, $\mathbf{C}_{[a]_\lambda}$ is simple. 

Now we define the simple conservative minority tree $\mathcal{T} = (T, \leq_\mathcal{T}, M_\mathcal{T})$ with $\leq_\mathcal{T}$ equal to the subset relation and $M_\mathcal{T} = (\fC_{[a]_\theta})_{a \in A}$ as defined above. We claim that the mapping $\phi \colon \fA \to \fA_\mathcal{T}$ defined by $\phi(a) := \{a\}$ is an isomorphism. Indeed, take $a,b,c \in A$. We want to show that $\phi(m(a,b,c)) = m_\mathcal{T}(\{a\},\{b\},\{c\})$. This is obvious if $a = b = c$, so suppose without loss of generality that $a \neq b$. In this case, there exists a minimal nontrivial block congruence $\theta$ with $[a]_\theta = [b]_\theta = [c]_\theta$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:blocksarecongruences}). Notice that the nontrivial block is precisely equal to $\bigvee \{\{a\},\{b\},\{c\}\}$ in $T$. Let $\lambda$ be the maximal congruence of the subalgebra of $\fA$ with underlying set $[a]_\theta$. Then it is easy to see that 
for  $x \in \{a,b,c\}$: 
\begin{align*}
m(a,b,c) &= x \\
\iff m([a]_\lambda, [b]_\lambda, [c]_\lambda) &= [x]_\lambda
\\
\iff m_\mathcal{T}(\{a\},\{b\},\{c\}) &= \{x\}. 
\end{align*}

Finally, it is obvious that $\mathcal{T}$ is reduced, because $[a]_\theta$ is not a leaf in $\mathcal{T}$ if and only if $|[a]_\theta| > 1$ if and only if $[a]_\theta$ has a maximal congruence. The last condition guarantees that all vertices of $\mathcal{T}$ that are not leaves have at least two children. 
This finishes the proof of the lemma.
\end{proof}

