\subsubsection{Uniqueness of representations}
The following is essentially a consequence of Lemma~\ref{lemma:congruencesoftreealgebras} and Theorem~\ref{thm:representwithtreealgebras}.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:simplereducedrepresentationsareunique}
    Any two simple and reduced tree representations of a conservative minority algebra $\fA$ are isomorphic. 
\end{lemma}
    \begin{proof}
        Let $\mathcal{S}$ be a simple reduced tree representation of $\fA$ and let $\mathcal{T}$ be the tree constructed in Theorem~\ref{thm:representwithtreealgebras}. We have that $\fA$ and $\fA_\mathcal{S}$ are isomorphic. Lemma~\ref{lemma:congruencesoftreealgebras} indicates that the mapping \red{$v \mapsto \; \sim_v$} is surjective onto the set of block congruences of $\fA_\mathcal{S}$, which is in bijective corresponence with the set of block congruences of $\fA$. If distinct vertices $v_1, v_2$ of $\mathcal{S}$ are such that $\sim_{v_1} = \; \sim_{v_2}$, then both $v_1$ and $v_2$ are leaves of $\mathcal{S}$. Indeed, if at least one of $v_1, v_2$ is not a leaf, then at least one $\sim_{v_1}, \sim_{v_2}$ has a nontrivial block, because we assume that $\mathcal{S}$ is reduced. It follows that both $\sim_{v_1}, \sim_{v_2}$ have a nontrivial block, because we assume they are equal. The only way this can happen is if $v_1$ and $v_2$ are comparable in $\mathcal{S}$. It is easy to see, for distinct vertices $v_1, v_2$ that are not leaves in a reduced tree, that $\sim_{v_1}$ and $\sim_{v_2}$ are distinct as well. Therefore, the set of vertices of $\mathcal{S}$ and the equivalence classes of block congruences of $\fA$ are in bijective correspondence. It is now not hard to see that this bijection is an isomorphism between $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{T}$. 
%\blue{ToDo: There is an easier way to see this, just take two nonisomorphic simple reduced trees and show they represent nonisomorphic algebras.}\todo{MB: do you want to implement this?}
    \end{proof}

\subsubsection{Subtrees}
Let ${\mathcal T}$ be a conservative minority tree.
Given a set of vertices $S \subseteq T$, we define the \emph{conservative minority subtree}  $\mathcal{T}_S = (T_S, \leq_{\mathcal{T}_S}, M_{\mathcal{T}_S}\}$ of ${\mathcal T}$ to be the conservative minority tree determined by the following data:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $(T_{S},\leq_{{\bT}_S})$ is the subtree of $(T,\leq_{\bT})$ spanned by $S$ and the root of \red{$\mathcal T$}, and 
   % REDUNDANT BY PREVIOUS ITEM: 
    %\item $\leq_{\mathcal{T}_{S}} $ is the restriction of $ \leq_\mathcal{T}$ to $T_S$, and
    \item $M_{\mathcal{T}_S} = (\mathbf{C}_{v,\mathcal{T}_{S}})_{v\in T_S \setminus L_{T_S}}$, where $\mathbf{C}_{v, \mathcal{T}_{S}}$ is the restriction of $\mathbf{C}_{v,\mathcal{T}}$ to $C_{v,\mathcal{T}_{S}}$.  
\end{itemize}

A subtree ${\mathcal T}_S$ of ${\mathcal T}$ is called a \emph{proper subtree} if 
$S \neq T$.
We begin to develop the connection between conservative minority trees and the algebras they represent with the following easy lemma.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:subalgebrarepresentingtree}
    Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a conservative minority tree and let $S \subseteq L_\mathcal{T}$. Then $S$ is the domain of a subalgebra $\mathbf{S} \leq \fA_\mathcal{T}$ which is represented by $\mathcal{T}_S$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Since $\fA_\mathcal{T}$ is conservative, it is immediate that $S$ is the domain of a subalgebra $\mathbf{S} \leq \fA_\mathcal{T}$. It is also immediate that $\mathcal{T}_S$ represents $\mathbf{S}$, since 

    \begin{itemize}
    \item
    the leaves of $\mathcal{T}_S$ are exactly the elements of $S$, 
    \item $T_S$ contains all least upper bounds of subsets of $S$, and 
    \item for $a,b,c \in L_{\mathcal{T}_S}$ and $v = \bigvee \{a,b,c\}$, only the elements of \red{$\mathcal{T}_S$} are used to compute $m_{v,\mathcal{T}}(a,b,c)$. \qedhere 
    \end{itemize}
\end{proof}

In general, the simplicity of a conservative minority tree $\mathcal{T}$ need not imply the simplicity of the conservative minority tree $\mathcal{T}_S$ which represents the subalgebra $\mathbf{S} \leq \fA_\mathcal{T}$. Indeed, there might exist a local algebra $\mathbf{C}_{v,\mathcal{T}_S} \leq \mathbf{C}_{v, \mathcal{T}}$ which is no longer simple and this situation happens whenever $\mathbf{C}_{v, \mathcal{T}}$ is simple but not hereditarily simple. The fact that simplicity is not always inherited by subtrees highlights the complexity of the structures that we wish to understand and in general there is no hope to obtain from the congruences of an algebra a complete description of its invariant relations. However, hereditary simplicity is inherited by subtrees of conservative minority trees whose local algebras are all hereditarily simple. This follows directly from the definitions. Since the only hereditarily simple conservative minority algebras we study in this paper are the projection conservative minority algebras, we record this fact in the following remark. 

\begin{remark}\label{rem:subtreesofhsimpletreesarehsimple}
If $\mathcal{T}$ is a projection conservative minority tree, then $\mathcal{T}_S$ is also a projection conservative minority tree for any subtree $\mathcal{T}_S$.
\end{remark}

The subdirectly irreducible subalgebras of conservative minority algebras play a crucial role in this paper. Here we apply Proposition \ref{prop:subdirectlyirreduciblearelinearchains} to obtain a characterization of those subtrees of conservative minority trees which represent subdirectly irreducible subalgebras of the leaf algebra. We call a conservative minority tree $\mathcal{T}$ a \emph{sapling} if for all $v \in T$, there is at most one $w \in C_v$ so that $\{ u : u \leq_\mathcal{T} w \}$ is not a linearly ordered chain.
A \emph{sapling of $\mathcal{T}$} is a conservative minority subtree of $\mathcal{T}$ which is a sapling. If $\mathcal{T}$ is a conservative minority tree, we say that a sapling $\mathcal{T}_S$ of $\mathcal{T}$ is 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{full} if ${\bf C}_{v, \mathcal{T}_S} = {\bf C}_{v, \mathcal{T}}$ for every $v \in {\mathcal T}_S$ with $|C_v| \geq 2$; 
    %A \red{sapling of $\mathcal{T}$ is 
\item \emph{maximal} if there is no 
sapling of $\mathcal{T}$ which has 
$\mathcal{T}_S$ as a proper subtree.
\end{itemize}
Note that for a sapling $\mathcal{T}_S$, the set of vertices $\{v: |C_v| \geq 2\}$ is linearly ordered by $\leq_\mathcal{T}$. We call this linearly ordered set of vertices the \emph{trunk} of the sapling $\mathcal{T}_S$ and denote this set by $\Trunk(\mathcal{T}_S)$. When the sapling representing some subdirectly irreducible conservative minority algebra $\mathbf{A}$ is clear, we may simply write $\Trunk(\fA)$ instead. Note that the isomorphism class of the algebra represented by a full sapling is uniquely determined by its trunk. We define the \emph{height} of a subdirectly irreducible conservative minority algebra $\fA$ to be the size of the trunk of any tree representing it (note that this is an invariant of the algebra). If a subalgebra $\mathbf{S} \leq \mathbf{A}_\mathcal{T}$ is represented by a full sapling $\mathcal{T}_S$, we say that $\mathbf{S}$ is a \emph{full subalgebra}. An isomorphism graph $G \leq \mathbf{S}_1 \times \mathbf{S}_2$ is called a \emph{full isomorphism graph} if both $\mathbf{S}_1$ and $\mathbf{S}_2$ are full. 
Figure \ref{fig:saplings} provides some pictures of these definitions. 

\begin{remark}\label{rem:emptytrunk}
    We emphasize that a sapling $\mathcal{T}$ can have empty trunk, in which case its underlying tree structure is a linearly ordered chain. Obviously, a subdirectly irreducible algebra represented by a sapling with empty trunk is a trivial one element algebra. 
\end{remark}


