\begin{lemma}\label{lem:saplingsrepresentsubdirectirreducible}
Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a conservative minority tree, let $\mathbf{S} \leq \fA_\mathcal{T}$, and let $\mathcal{T}_S$ be the conservative minority subtree of $\mathcal{T}$ as defined above. The following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\mathbf{S}$ is subdirectly irreducible, then $\mathcal{T}_S$ is a sapling.
\item If $\mathcal{T}$ is a simple conservative minority tree, then every full sapling of $\mathcal{T}$  represents a subdirectly irreducible algebra.
\item If $\mathcal{T}$ is a projection conservative minority tree, then every subtree of $\mathcal{T}$ is a projection conservative minority tree and every sapling of $\mathcal{T}$  represents a subdirectly irreducible algebra. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    To establish 1., suppose that $\mathcal{T}_S$ is not a sapling. Then there exists $v \in T_S$ with children $w_1, w_2$ so that the each of the sets $\{u : u \leq w_1 \}$ and $\{ u: u \leq w_2\}$ is not a linearly ordered chain. This implies that each  of $\sim_{w_1}$ and $\sim_{w_2}$ is a nontrivial block congruence of $\fA_{\mathcal{T}_S}$, but they are incomparable because $w_1$ and $w_2$ are incomparable.  Proposition \ref{prop:subdirectlyirreduciblearelinearchains} indicates that the congruences of $\mathbf{S}$ are block congruences that are linearly ordered by inclusion, so $\mathbf{S}$ is not subdirectly irreducible. 

    To establish 2., note that if $\mathcal{T}$ is a simple conservative minority tree, then so is any full sapling $\mathcal{T}_S$, because by definition every local algebra $\mathbf{C}_{v, \mathcal{T}_S}$ is either a simple one element algebra or the simple algebra $\mathbf{C}_{v, \mathcal{T}}$. By Lemma \ref{lemma:congruencesoftreealgebras}, the block congruences of $\mathbf{S}$ are all of the form $\sim_v$ for $v \in T_S$. Note that $\sim_v$ is the equality relation if $\{u: u \leq v\}$ is a linearly ordered chain. Since $\mathcal{T}_S$ is a sapling, it follows that the set of congruences $\{\sim_v: v \in T_S\}$ is linearly ordered by inclusion. Proposition \ref{prop:subdirectlyirreduciblearelinearchains} now indicates that $\mathbf{S}$ is subdirectly irreducible. 

    To establish 3., take a sapling $\mathcal{T}_S$ of $\mathcal{T}$. By Remark \ref{rem:subtreesofhsimpletreesarehsimple}, we know that $\mathcal{T}_S$ is a projection conservative minority tree. Since $\mathcal{T}_S$ is a full sapling of itself, it follows from 2. above that $\fA_{\mathcal{T}_S}$ subdirectly irreducible.
\end{proof}


\subsubsection{Tree transformations}\label{sec:treetransformations}
We can obtain the (up to isomorphism unique) simple reduced tree representing a given conservative minority algebra $\fA$ 
from any other representing tree by applying a sequence of local transformations.
%\todo{Where is this needed in the present paper?} 
If each transformation preserves the algebra that is represented and a simple reduced tree is produced, it must be isomorphic to any other simple reduced representing tree. There are only two transformations that are needed. 

The first transformation is identifying an only child and its parent. Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a conservative minority tree and suppose that there exist vertices $v, w$ of $\mathcal{T}$ with $w$ the unique child of $v$. We define $\mathcal{T}^{v=w} = (T^{v=w}, \leq_{\mathcal{T}^{v=w}}, M_{\mathcal{T}^{v=w}})$, where
\begin{itemize}
    \item $T^{v=w} = T\setminus \{v\}$,
    \item $\leq_{\mathcal{T}^{v=w}}$ is the restriction of $\leq_\mathcal{T}$ to $T\setminus \{v\}$, and
    \item $M_{\mathcal{T}^{v=w}} = M_\mathcal{T} \setminus \{ \mathbf{C}_v\}$.
\end{itemize}
Since $v$ has a single child $w$, it is impossible for the join of some vertices in $\mathcal{T}$ to equal $v$ unless one of them is equal to $v$. Therefore, $\fA_{\mathcal{T}^{v=w}} = \fA_\mathcal{T}$.

The second transformation refines a given $\mathcal{T}$ by introducing additional vertices representing classes of a nontrivial proper congruence. Suppose that $v$ is a vertex of $\mathcal{T}$ such that $\mathbf{C}_v$ is not a simple algebra. There exists a proper congruence $\lambda$ of $\mathbf{C}_v$ with classes $w_1, \dots, w_k$ for some $k \leq 3$. We define a new tree $\mathcal{T}^{\lambda \to v}$, where 
\begin{itemize}
    \item $T^{\lambda \to v} = T \cup \{w_1, \dots, w_k\} $,
    \item $\leq_{\mathcal{T}^{\lambda \to v}} $ is the transitive closure of $\leq_\mathcal{T} \cup 
   \left( \bigcup_{1\leq i \leq k} \{(c,w_i): c\in w_i\} \right)\cup \left(\bigcup_{1\leq i \leq k} \{(w_i, v)\} \right)$, and
   \item $M_{\mathcal{T}^{\lambda \to v}}$ contains all of $M_\mathcal{T}$, except for $\mathbf{C}_v$, which is updated to $\mathbf{C}_v/\lambda$. It also contains for each $1\leq i \leq k$ the algebra $\mathbf{C}_{w_i} = \mathbf{C}_v\vert_{w_i}$, the restriction of $\mathbf{C}_v$ to the class $w_i$.
\end{itemize}
This transformation also does not impact the represented algebra, that is, we have that $\fA_{\mathcal{T}^{\lambda \to v }} = \fA_\mathcal{T}$. The proof is another routine application of the definitions. 
