\subsubsection{Basic tree operations}
We conclude this section with some notation for some basic tree constructions. 
We will also have frequent need to attach the root of one conservative minority tree to a particular leaf of another conservative minority tree. While it is easy to informally describe this new tree, we need to be careful in practice, since the two trees involved will often have a nonempty intersection and may also share some local conservative minority structure. Therefore, we require a notation that is formally correct, yet does not obfuscate when two local conservative minority algebras are isomorphic. Our solution to this is to represent tree vertices as sequences over some finite set which encode partial branches. 







Let $X$ be a nonempty finite set. We set $X^{< \omega} = \{ v\in X^i: i < \omega \}$ to be the set of all finite sequences over $X$. The set of sequences $X^{< \omega}$ is equipped with a tree order $\leq$, where $v \leq w$ if and only if $w$ is an initial segment of $v$, with the empty sequence, which we denote by $\epsilon$, as unique maximal element (note that we have reversed the usual order). We say that $\mathcal{T} = (T, \leq_\mathcal{T}, M_\mathcal{T})$ is a \emph{conservative minority branch tree over $X$} if the tree $(T, \leq_\mathcal{T})$ is a finite subtree of $X^{< \omega}$ for some finite $X$.

In the context of conservative minority branch  trees, we will allow ourselves the following abuse of notation. Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a conservative minority branch  tree and let $v = (v_0, \dots, v_{k-1}) \in T \setminus L_v$. Our formalism indicates that the set $C_v$ is the set of all length $k+1$ sequences in $T$ that extend $v$, i.e., that 
\[
C_v = \{(w_0, \dots, w_{k-1}, w_k) \in T: \text{$w_i = v_i$ for all $0 \leq i \leq k-1$}  \}.
\]
Since all of the children of $v$ contain $v$ as a prefix, it is convenient to instead take
\[
C_v = \{a: (v_0, \dots, v_{k-1},a) \in T\}.
\]



Now let $\mathcal{T} = (T, \leq_\mathcal{T}, M_\mathcal{T})$ and $\mathcal{S} = (S, \leq_\mathcal{S}, M_\mathcal{S})$ be two conservative minority branch  trees over $X$ and $Y$, respectively. For $v \in \mathcal{T}$, we define
\[
\mathcal{T} \oplus_v \mathcal{S} = ( T \oplus_v S, \leq_{\mathcal{T} \oplus_v \mathcal{S}}, M_{\mathcal{T} \oplus_v \mathcal{S}})
\]
to be the conservative minority branch  tree over $X \cup Y$ defined by the following data:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $T \oplus_v S = (T \setminus \{v\}) \cup \{  v^\frown w: w \in \mathcal{S}\}$, 
    \item $\leq_{\mathcal{T} \oplus_v \mathcal{S}} $ is equal to the tree order on $(X\cup Y)^{< \omega}$, and
    \item $M_{\mathcal{T} \oplus_v \mathcal{S}} = M_{\mathcal{T}}\setminus \{\mathbf{C}_v\} \cup \{ \mathbf{C}_{v^\frown w}: w \in S \setminus L_\mathcal{S} \}$, where $\mathbf{C}_{v^\frown w} = \mathbf{C}_w \in M_\mathcal{S}$.
\end{itemize}



We introduce more notation that will be convenient later. Given any vertex $v$ of $\mathcal{T}$ of a conservative minority branch  tree, we denote by $\mathcal{T}^{\leq v} = (T^{\leq v}, \leq, M_{\mathcal{T}^{\leq v}})$ the conservative minority tree defined by the following data:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $T^{ \leq v} = \{ w: w^\frown v \in T \}$, 
    \item $\leq$ is the same order as for $\mathcal{T}$, and
    \item $M_{\mathcal{T}^{\leq v}} = (\mathbf{C}_{w, \mathcal{T}^{\leq v}})_{w \in T^{\leq v} \setminus L_{\mathcal{T}^{\leq v}}}$, where $\mathbf{C}_{w, \mathcal{T}^{\leq v}} = \mathbf{C}_{v^\frown w, \mathcal{T}}$.
\end{itemize}
Informally, $\mathcal{T}^{\leq v}$ is just $\mathcal{T}$ restricted to the downset determined by $v$. With a similar notation, we denote by $\mathcal{T}^{\nleq v}$ the conservative minority tree obtained from $\mathcal{T}$ obtained by pruning all vertices less than or equal to $v$ from $\mathcal{T}$. We denote by $\mathcal{T}^{\nless v}$ the conservative minority tree obtained from $\mathcal{T}$ be pruning all vertices that are strictly below $v$. 
%TO MANUEL: These definitions make sense when $\mathcal{T}$ is not specifically a conservative minority branch  tree, but there is a formal subtlety that we wish to avoid. If we defined $\mathcal{T}^{\leq v}$ for conservative minority trees that are not conservative minority branch  trees, then specializing this definition to conservative minority branch  trees would necessitate that $v$ is a prefix of all vertices of $\mathcal{T}^{\leq v}$ and deleting this prefix every time before performing a tree gluing would be painful. 
The following lemma easily follows from the definitions.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:treerepresentationofquotients}
    Let $\mathcal{T}$ be a conservative minority branch  tree and let $v$ be a vertex of $\mathcal{T}$. The following hold:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathcal{T} = \mathcal{T}^{\nless v} \oplus_v \mathcal{T}^{\leq v} $
    \item
    The function 
    $\phi_v \colon L_{\mathcal{T}} \to L_{\mathcal{T}^{\nless v}}$ defined by 
    \[
    \phi(w) = 
    \begin{cases}
        v & \text{ if $w \leq_\mathcal{T} v$}\\
        w & \text{ otherwise}
    \end{cases}
    \]
   is a homomorphism from $\fA_\mathcal{T}$ to $\fA_{\mathcal{T}^{\nless v}}$ with kernel $\sim_v$. 
   \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
