\subsection{Invariant relations}
\label{sect:inv-rels}
In this section we will produce for an arbitrary finite conservative minority algebra $\fA$ a  finite set of relations whose primitive positive closure equals $\Inv(\fA)$. 

\begin{remark}
    %Before we continue our work, we mention some of the history behind the ideas of this section. Underlying this analysis is a deep generalization of the concept of abelianness from groups to arbitrary algebras of any functional signature which has been a fundamental tool in Universal Algebra since the 1970's. In fact, the concept of an abelian algebra is piece of a much broader commutator theory which was first discovered for Maltsev varieties of algebras by Smith\todo{references missing; some survey?} and subsequently refined and generalized by Hagemann, Hermann, Freese, McKenzie, Taylor, Kearnes, Szendrei, Kiss, and many more. Broadly speaking, the commutator collapses the obstacles an algebra has which prevent it from possessing a module representation, and hence the theory can transform questions about general algebraic structures into questions about modules. This approach is most successful in the context of so-called congruence modular varieties; the interested reader can consult the book of Freese and McKenzie~\cite{FreeseMcKenzie} for a deep analysis of the commutator in this setting. 
Kearnes and Szendrei~\cite{KearnesSzendrei-Parallologram} apply some of the deeper aspects of the modular commutator theory to obtain a nice set of relations for an algebra with a parallelogram term, which is a generalization of a Maltsev operation which characterizes the property of having `few subpowers'~\cite{IMMVW}. 
